{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "07-definite-integrals.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Area and the fundamental theorem",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the area under a curve: first approximately, with rectangles, then exactly, from an antiderivative, and see why an antiderivative gives it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What is the area under $y = x^2$ from $0$ to $1$? Geometry has formulas for rectangles and triangles, not for a curved edge. It is less than $\\frac12$, the triangle under the diagonal $y = x$, since the curve stays below the diagonal. Guess a number before reading on.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Rectangles",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Rectangles we can add. Cut the region into strips, and replace each strip by a rectangle as tall as the curve at the strip's right edge:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -0.1, 1.15, -0.1, 1.1\nF = graph(x^2, x, 0, 1) thick color 2\nU = poly(0, 1*1/16 + i*(1*1/16)^2, 2*1/16 + i*(2*1/16)^2, 3*1/16 + i*(3*1/16)^2, 4*1/16 + i*(4*1/16)^2, 5*1/16 + i*(5*1/16)^2, 6*1/16 + i*(6*1/16)^2, 7*1/16 + i*(7*1/16)^2, 8*1/16 + i*(8*1/16)^2, 9*1/16 + i*(9*1/16)^2, 10*1/16 + i*(10*1/16)^2, 11*1/16 + i*(11*1/16)^2, 12*1/16 + i*(12*1/16)^2, 13*1/16 + i*(13*1/16)^2, 14*1/16 + i*(14*1/16)^2, 15*1/16 + i*(15*1/16)^2, 16*1/16 + i*(16*1/16)^2, 1) color 2\nA1 = poly(0/5, 1/5, 1/5 + 1/25*i, 0/5 + 1/25*i) color 1\nA2 = poly(1/5, 2/5, 2/5 + 4/25*i, 1/5 + 4/25*i) color 1\nA3 = poly(2/5, 3/5, 3/5 + 9/25*i, 2/5 + 9/25*i) color 1\nA4 = poly(3/5, 4/5, 4/5 + 16/25*i, 3/5 + 16/25*i) color 1\nA5 = poly(4/5, 5/5, 5/5 + 25/25*i, 4/5 + 25/25*i) color 1\nB1 = poly(0/10, 1/10, 1/10 + 1/100*i, 0/10 + 1/100*i) color 4\nB2 = poly(1/10, 2/10, 2/10 + 4/100*i, 1/10 + 4/100*i) color 4\nB3 = poly(2/10, 3/10, 3/10 + 9/100*i, 2/10 + 9/100*i) color 4\nB4 = poly(3/10, 4/10, 4/10 + 16/100*i, 3/10 + 16/100*i) color 4\nB5 = poly(4/10, 5/10, 5/10 + 25/100*i, 4/10 + 25/100*i) color 4\nB6 = poly(5/10, 6/10, 6/10 + 36/100*i, 5/10 + 36/100*i) color 4\nB7 = poly(6/10, 7/10, 7/10 + 49/100*i, 6/10 + 49/100*i) color 4\nB8 = poly(7/10, 8/10, 8/10 + 64/100*i, 7/10 + 64/100*i) color 4\nB9 = poly(8/10, 9/10, 9/10 + 81/100*i, 8/10 + 81/100*i) color 4\nB10 = poly(9/10, 10/10, 10/10 + 100/100*i, 9/10 + 100/100*i) color 4\nC1 = poly(0/20, 1/20, 1/20 + 1/400*i, 0/20 + 1/400*i) color 5\nC2 = poly(1/20, 2/20, 2/20 + 4/400*i, 1/20 + 4/400*i) color 5\nC3 = poly(2/20, 3/20, 3/20 + 9/400*i, 2/20 + 9/400*i) color 5\nC4 = poly(3/20, 4/20, 4/20 + 16/400*i, 3/20 + 16/400*i) color 5\nC5 = poly(4/20, 5/20, 5/20 + 25/400*i, 4/20 + 25/400*i) color 5\nC6 = poly(5/20, 6/20, 6/20 + 36/400*i, 5/20 + 36/400*i) color 5\nC7 = poly(6/20, 7/20, 7/20 + 49/400*i, 6/20 + 49/400*i) color 5\nC8 = poly(7/20, 8/20, 8/20 + 64/400*i, 7/20 + 64/400*i) color 5\nC9 = poly(8/20, 9/20, 9/20 + 81/400*i, 8/20 + 81/400*i) color 5\nC10 = poly(9/20, 10/20, 10/20 + 100/400*i, 9/20 + 100/400*i) color 5\nC11 = poly(10/20, 11/20, 11/20 + 121/400*i, 10/20 + 121/400*i) color 5\nC12 = poly(11/20, 12/20, 12/20 + 144/400*i, 11/20 + 144/400*i) color 5\nC13 = poly(12/20, 13/20, 13/20 + 169/400*i, 12/20 + 169/400*i) color 5\nC14 = poly(13/20, 14/20, 14/20 + 196/400*i, 13/20 + 196/400*i) color 5\nC15 = poly(14/20, 15/20, 15/20 + 225/400*i, 14/20 + 225/400*i) color 5\nC16 = poly(15/20, 16/20, 16/20 + 256/400*i, 15/20 + 256/400*i) color 5\nC17 = poly(16/20, 17/20, 17/20 + 289/400*i, 16/20 + 289/400*i) color 5\nC18 = poly(17/20, 18/20, 18/20 + 324/400*i, 17/20 + 324/400*i) color 5\nC19 = poly(18/20, 19/20, 19/20 + 361/400*i, 18/20 + 361/400*i) color 5\nC20 = poly(19/20, 20/20, 20/20 + 400/400*i, 19/20 + 400/400*i) color 5\n> show F, U | The curve $y = x^2$ from $0$ to $1$, and the area under it: the number we want.\n> show A1, A2, A3, A4, A5 | Five strips of width $\\frac15$; the $k$-th rectangle is $(k/5)^2$ tall. Each one pokes above the curve, so together they are too big.\n> hide A1, A2, A3, A4, A5; show B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6, B7, B8, B9, B10 | Ten strips: the slivers above the curve are thinner, and the total is closer.\n> hide B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6, B7, B8, B9, B10; show C1, C2, C3, C4, C5, C6, C7, C8, C9, C10, C11, C12, C13, C14, C15, C16, C17, C18, C19, C20 | Twenty: the slivers thin again. Each doubling of the strips about halves what pokes out.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Five rectangles of width $\\frac15$ and heights $\\left(\\frac15\\right)^2, \\left(\\frac25\\right)^2, \\dots, \\left(\\frac55\\right)^2$. `sum(f, k, a, b)` adds $f$ for $k = a, a + 1, \\dots, b$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(k^2, k, 1, 5)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum((k/5)^2 / 5, k, 1, 5)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\frac{11}{25} = 0.44$: too big, as the picture said. More strips poke out less.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Right Riemann sum, definite integral\n> Cut $[0, 1]$ into $n$ strips of width $\\frac1n$ and stand a rectangle of height $f(\\frac kn)$ on the $k$-th: the area of the rectangles is $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f\\!\\left(\\tfrac kn\\right) \\tfrac1n$. For a continuous $f$ these sums approach a single number as $n$ grows: the **definite integral** $\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$, the area under the curve. On $[a, b]$ it is the same, with strips of width $\\frac{b - a}{n}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let n = 10",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "slider": {
        "min": 1,
        "max": 60,
        "step": 1
      },
      "mode": "raw"
    },
    {
      "src": "let R = sum((k/n)^2 / n, k, 1, n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(R)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Drag `n`. The sum is an exact fraction for every $n$, and its decimal falls toward a number from above: the rectangles stand above the curve. Which number? Even at $n = 60$ it is still $0.34\\ldots$, so the sums alone are slow to say.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The area so far",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For the exact value, change the question: not the area up to $1$, but the area up to any $x$. Call it $A(x)$, the area so far. How fast does it grow as $x$ moves?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock X from 0.6 to 1.5\nview -0.1, 1.6, -0.15, 2.4\nF = graph(x^2, x, 0, 1.5) color 2\nLF = label(0.6 + 1.6*i, \"f(x) = x^2\") color 2\nU = poly(0, 1*X/16 + i*(1*X/16)^2, 2*X/16 + i*(2*X/16)^2, 3*X/16 + i*(3*X/16)^2, 4*X/16 + i*(4*X/16)^2, 5*X/16 + i*(5*X/16)^2, 6*X/16 + i*(6*X/16)^2, 7*X/16 + i*(7*X/16)^2, 8*X/16 + i*(8*X/16)^2, 9*X/16 + i*(9*X/16)^2, 10*X/16 + i*(10*X/16)^2, 11*X/16 + i*(11*X/16)^2, 12*X/16 + i*(12*X/16)^2, 13*X/16 + i*(13*X/16)^2, 14*X/16 + i*(14*X/16)^2, 15*X/16 + i*(15*X/16)^2, 16*X/16 + i*(16*X/16)^2, X) color 2\nA = graph(x^3/3, x, 0, X) thick color 1\nP = point(X + i*X^3/3) color 1\nLA = label(P, \"A(x)\") color 1\nS = poly(X, X + 0.1, X + 0.1 + i*X^2, X + i*X^2) color 3\n> show F, LF, U | The curve $y = x^2$, and the region under it from $0$ to $x$.\n> show A, P, LA; play X to 1.5 in 5s | Move $x$ and plot the area so far, $A(x)$, as it grows.\n> show S; play X to 1 in 3s | Push $x$ on by $dx$: the area gains a thin strip, of height $f(x)$ and width $dx$. So $dA \\approx f(x)\\,dx$: the slope of $A$ is $f$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Push $x$ on by $dx$ and $A$ grows by a strip of height $f(x)$ and width $dx$, area about $f(x)\\,dx$ (the sliver between the strip's top and the curve is smaller still, of the order of $dx^2$). So $dA \\approx f(x)\\,dx$: the slope of the area so far is the height of the curve, $A' = f$. The area so far is an antiderivative, and lesson 6 finds those.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For $f = x^2$: $A(x) = \\frac{x^3}{3} + C$, and $A(0) = 0$, since there is no area yet, so $C = 0$ and the area up to $1$ is $\\frac13$. Any antiderivative $F$ gives the same difference $F(1) - F(0)$: the $C$ cancels.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Fundamental theorem of calculus\n> If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$ and $F' = f$ there, then $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`integrate(f, x, a, b)` finds a checked antiderivative, then evaluates it at the bounds. Step through it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2, x, 0, 1)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\frac13 = 0.333\\ldots$: the number the Riemann sums were falling toward.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Signed area",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One arch of the sine wave, from $0$ to $\\pi$, has area exactly $2$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(sin(x), x, 0, pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Twice the interval is not twice the area. From $0$ to $2\\pi$ the second arch lies below the axis, where $f < 0$: the strips $f(x)\\,dx$ count as negative, and the two arches cancel. A definite integral is a *signed* area.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "view -0.3, 6.6, -1.3, 1.3\nG = graph(sin(x), x, 0, 2pi) thick color 2\nP = poly(pi*0/16 + i*sin(pi*0/16), pi*1/16 + i*sin(pi*1/16), pi*2/16 + i*sin(pi*2/16), pi*3/16 + i*sin(pi*3/16), pi*4/16 + i*sin(pi*4/16), pi*5/16 + i*sin(pi*5/16), pi*6/16 + i*sin(pi*6/16), pi*7/16 + i*sin(pi*7/16), pi*8/16 + i*sin(pi*8/16), pi*9/16 + i*sin(pi*9/16), pi*10/16 + i*sin(pi*10/16), pi*11/16 + i*sin(pi*11/16), pi*12/16 + i*sin(pi*12/16), pi*13/16 + i*sin(pi*13/16), pi*14/16 + i*sin(pi*14/16), pi*15/16 + i*sin(pi*15/16), pi*16/16 + i*sin(pi*16/16)) color 1\nN = poly(pi*16/16 + i*sin(pi*16/16), pi*17/16 + i*sin(pi*17/16), pi*18/16 + i*sin(pi*18/16), pi*19/16 + i*sin(pi*19/16), pi*20/16 + i*sin(pi*20/16), pi*21/16 + i*sin(pi*21/16), pi*22/16 + i*sin(pi*22/16), pi*23/16 + i*sin(pi*23/16), pi*24/16 + i*sin(pi*24/16), pi*25/16 + i*sin(pi*25/16), pi*26/16 + i*sin(pi*26/16), pi*27/16 + i*sin(pi*27/16), pi*28/16 + i*sin(pi*28/16), pi*29/16 + i*sin(pi*29/16), pi*30/16 + i*sin(pi*30/16), pi*31/16 + i*sin(pi*31/16), pi*32/16 + i*sin(pi*32/16)) color 4\nLP = label(pi/2 + 0.4*i, \"+2\") color 1\nLN = label(3*pi/2 - 0.5*i, \"-2\") color 4\n> show G, P, LP | From $0$ to $\\pi$ the arch is above the axis: its strips $f(x)\\,dx$ are positive, and it counts $+2$.\n> show N, LN | From $\\pi$ to $2\\pi$ it is below: its strips are negative, and it counts $-2$. Together, $0$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(sin(x), x, 0, 2pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x, x, 0, 2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x\\,dx$. (Check it against the triangle it is the area of.)",
      "hints": [
        "An antiderivative is $\\frac{x^2}{2}$; evaluate at $2$ and at $0$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2, x, 1, 3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_1^3 x^2\\,dx$.",
      "hints": [
        "$F(x) = \\frac{x^3}{3}$; the answer is $F(3) - F(1)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^3, x, 0, 1)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Invent it: what is the area under $y = x^3$ from $0$ to $1$? Find the area-so-far function first.",
      "hints": [
        "$A' = x^3$ and $A(0) = 0$.",
        "$A(x) = \\frac{x^4}{4}$; the area is $A(1)$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(2k - 1, k, 1, 6)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Add the first six odd numbers, $1 + 3 + 5 + \\dots + 11$.",
      "hints": [
        "Try $1$, then $1 + 3$, then $1 + 3 + 5$: what do the totals have in common?",
        "Lesson 1's growing square: from side $k - 1$ to side $k$ it gains two strips and a corner, $2(k - 1) + 1 = 2k - 1$ unit squares."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Area is the number rectangle sums approach. The area so far grows at the rate of the curve's height, so it is an antiderivative, and the area from $a$ to $b$ is $F(b) - F(a)$, signed. Slope and area undo each other: that is the fundamental theorem, and the two halves of this course are one subject.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
